Other methods
In addition to the frequently adopted approaches for target discovery of small-molecule probes for unlabeled NPs, there are several other approaches, including molecular docking, quantitative proteomics, phage technology, affinity chromatography, and protein microarrays.
Phage technology is applicable to bioactive peptide screening and the identification of small-molecule probe targets in NPs. The biological functions and therapeutic potential of small molecules can be revealed through specific binding peptide screening. A biotinylated paclitaxel derivative was used in a study by Rodi et al. [
87
]. Human Bcl-2 protein was successfully identified as a potential target of paclitaxel using a phage-displayed peptide library screening technique. Sequence similarity analysis and enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) experimental validation demonstrated that paclitaxel may exert its therapeutic effect by inducing apoptosis in tumor cells by directly binding to the Bcl-2 protein, providing a theoretical basis for the development of a new generation of anti-tumor drugs targeting Bcl-2 [
87
]. Takakusagi et al. [
88
] successfully prepared the biotinylated camptothecin derivative CPT-20-B. Screening of human leukocyte complementary DNA (cDNA) libraries using T7 phage display technology identified a calcium-binding protein sequence, NSSQSARR, which can bind specifically to CPT-20-B. Forward and reverse affinity assays confirmed the high affinity of calcium-binding protein for CPT-20-B, suggesting that it may be a new therapeutic target for camptothecin drugs, which provides a new direction for the research and development of anti-tumor drugs [
88
]. The potential target of ginsenoside Rh2 ((20S) G-Rh2) was identified as Annexin A2 by phage display technology [
89
]. The results of ITC showed that its binding constant was 1.13 × 10
−5
M
−1
, indicating moderate affinity. The observations that (20S) G-Rh2 inhibited Annexin A2-NF-κB p50 subunit interaction, blocked NF-κB activation and anti-apoptotic gene expression, and promoted apoptosis of cancer cells reveal a new strategy for the treatment of hepatocellular carcinoma and demonstrate the natural anti-cancer potential of (20S) G-Rh2 [
89
].
Protein microarray is a high-throughput protein analysis tool. Microarrays are constructed by arranging a large number of protein samples on a solid-phase surface to detect protein-molecule interactions and study protein functions and networks. A rat model of acute gouty arthritis was constructed. The expression levels of 90 proteins in synovial tissues of the rats were detected by using RayBio® human label-based antibody array I protein microarray. Proteins, such as the tumor necrosis factor-related apoptosis-inducing ligand and neuropilin-2, were upregulated in the rats, and the expression of these proteins could be downregulated by
Dioscorea dioscorea
, indicating the therapeutic potential of this NPs [
90
]. Another study used human proteomic microarrays and multiple validation methods to confirm that the target of silibinin was ferritin heavy chain 1 (FTH1). FTH1 regulates cellular iron metabolism, and silibinin inhibits nuclear receptor coactivator 4-FTH1-mediated ferritinophagy, reducing ferroptosis and effectively attenuating acute kidney injury caused by ischemia/reperfusion injury, demonstrating its potential as a treatment for ischemia/reperfusion injury and acute kidney injury [
56
].
Affinity chromatography is a separation technique based on specific intermolecular interactions that utilizes the high affinity between the ligand in the stationary phase (affinity matrix) and the target molecule (e.g., proteins, nucleic acids, and small molecules) to achieve separation. In one study, nearly 40 target proteins from ginsenosides were screened and validated using affinity chromatography, which confirmed that 20(S)-PPD binds directly to muscle-type creatine kinase (CK-MM), with binding constants of 2.53 ± 0.12 × 10
−5
M and 1.9 ± 0.30 × 10
−6
M. This study showed that PPD activated CK-MM, elevated phosphocreatine levels, optimized energy metabolism, and reduced lactate accumulation during exercise. In a weight-bearing swimming experiment, PPD significantly enhanced the exercise endurance of mice, confirming its effect of relieving fatigue. The finding opens a new avenue for the development of CK-MM activators based on dammarane-type triterpene structures [
91
]. Another study identified peroxiredoxin-2 (Prdx2) as the target protein of Cel using biotinylated Cel affinity capture combined with MS in BGC-823 and SGC-7901 gastric cancer cells [
92
]. The high affinity of binding (
K
D
, approximately 6.9 nM) indicates that Cel directly targets Prdx2 and induces apoptosis mediated by reactive oxygen species. These findings indicate the therapeutic potential of Prdx2 for gastric cancer and provide a new target for the development of drugs to treat gastric cancer [
92
].
Inflammation-associated proteins of the MAPK, NF-κB, and FoxO1/3 signaling pathways were identified in LPS-stimulated RAW264.7 cells using label-free quantitative proteomics [
93
]. The authors reported that carnosic acid significantly inhibited the phosphorylation of extracellular signal-regulated kinase 1/2, c-jun N-terminal kinase, p38 MAPKs, and NF-κB pathway proteins and prevented NF-κB activation. Carnosic acid also inhibited the expression of FoxO1/3 and promoted their degradation. The findings suggest that carnosic acid has therapeutic potential as an effective anti-inflammatory agent by inhibiting the aforementioned signaling pathways [
93
]. MT quantitative proteomics was used to analyze differentially expressed proteins in the serum of an adenovirus-TSH289-induced Graves' disease [
126
] mouse model [
94
]. FAS-associated death domain protein was identified as a key target of Xiehuo Xiaoying decoction in the treatment of Graves' disease. Immunohistochemistry and terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase-mediated dUTP nick end labeling staining further confirmed that Xiehuo Xiaoying decoction promoted the apoptosis of thyroid cells in the mouse model of Graves' disease by upregulating the expression of FAS-associated death domain protein, caspase 8, and caspase 3, with a therapeutic effect on Graves' disease [
94
].
Oridonin, an ent-kaurene diterpenoid with anti-inflammatory activity, specifically inhibits the assembly and activation of the NLR, and NLRP3 inflammasome by covalently binding to Cys279 in the NACHT structural domain of the NLRP3 protein, with anti-inflammatory effects through targeted mutagenesis. The findings implicate oridonin as a potential drug for treating NLRP3-related inflammatory and metabolic diseases [
95
].
With the increasing interest in complex biological systems and multi-component drugs, researchers need a method to identify the specific targets of action in these multimolecular systems. Although traditional target identification techniques are mainly used for single compounds, it is difficult to deal with complex mixtures of molecules, such as herbal extracts. The Zhao-Yao strategy has emerged to overcome the limitations of the existing techniques. This strategy is an innovative multimolecular systematic target identification method that immobilizes molecules on nanoparticles via photochemical cross-linking to capture target proteins in cell lysates. Using SPR technology, six anti-inflammatory target proteins (UBE2I, peroxiredoxin-6 (Prdx6), and RHOA) were rapidly screened from
Dendranthema indicum
extracts. Thirty-three major compounds had strong affinities for these target proteins in an LPS-induced BV2 microglial cell model, suggesting that the
D. indica
extract and its compounds have considerable therapeutic potential for inflammatory diseases [
96
]. The authors then constructed a photocrosslinking platform utilizing gold nanoparticles and 4,4′-dihydroxybenzophenone to accurately identify cellular targets in ultra-complex molecular systems, crosslinked phytochemicals to gold nanoparticles using an ultraviolet photocatalytic reaction, identified neuroprotective targets from brain tissues, and screened and verified the neuroprotective effects of target-binding compounds using Hsp60 as a target [
24
]. These findings provide an efficient strategy for the exploration of the pharmacological mechanism and active ingredients [
24
].
Other screening methods include molecular docking and SPR screening of TLR4, the target protein of the flavonoid baicalein in colorectal cancer cell lines, providing a new strategy for the use of novel TLR4-targeted therapeutic agents for the treatment of colorectal cancer [
97
]. SPR, cellular assays, and molecular docking techniques were utilized to screen for the binding of rutaecarpine to Keap1. Rutaecarpine significantly altered the symptoms in a model of colitis induced by dextran sulfate sodium, suggesting its' therapeutic potential for the treatment of inflammatory bowel disease [
98
]. Functional and structural screening techniques demonstrated that osthole significantly relieved airway constriction induced by acetylcholine and enhanced the diastolic effect of salbutamol, suggesting that it may be a new drug for the treatment of asthma [
127
]. Virtual screening and a main protein inhibitory activity assay based on fluorescence resonance energy transfer revealed that oridonin effectively inhibited the main protein activity of severe acute respiratory syndrome-coronavirus-2 (SARS-CoV-2) as well as SARS-CoV-2 polymerase and papain-like protease activities at high concentrations [
128
]. The findings demonstrate the broad-spectrum antiviral activity of oridonin and provide a theoretical and experimental basis for its use as a novel anti-SARS-CoV-2 drug [
128
]. Wang et al. [
129
] prepared magnetic nanoparticles enriched with surface benzophenone, which could effectively couple different drug molecules. The authors also demonstrated the potential application of this method in the preparation of NPs ingredient microarrays and targeted “hook fishing” [
129
].
